   199	Nobody talked to her.
   200	
   201	He watched it, because he could not have stood up to go elsewhere. Lira on her back on the road with her face turned away toward the ring, breathing through her teeth in the rhythm she used for a thing that hurt in the right places. Oryn's face going grey by the lantern as it had gone grey on a mountain over a leg, and greyer, and the sweat standing on it in the cold. Brom, sitting on the middle box — Seln had gone back sixty meters into the dark for it while the wall was still burning, and come out with it — with his right hand open on his knee and the fingers of it laid open across the pads by the string, bleeding slowly onto the box's worn corner. Karis beside him with the notebook open on her knees and both hands shaking, writing, because she could not say anything: her throat under the jaw was going dark on both sides in the shape of a mouth, and when she had tried to say a word at the stone what had come out was breath.
