   237	"Two hundred and forty," he said aloud. He had not known that he was still counting.
   238	
   239	"What's that?" said Brom. One of the pair had him by the skirt of the coat and was tugging. He let it tug.
   240	
   241	"Seln."
   242	
   243	The other one tried Cael. Its teeth met in the tail of his coat, and in the cloth of his breeches behind the knee, and it pulled, and stood back to see what he would do. He did nothing whatever except keep a head on a floor, and count.
   244	
   245	Seln came up the lane at two hundred and seventy.
   246	
   247	He was thirty inside his time. He came over the lip with a long thing under one arm, out of its cover, and no breath to speak with. He gave the bow to Brom, and stood holding his own knees.
   248	
   249	"There is a hook for the string," he said, when he could. "A healer does not draw that with her fingers. I did not stay to look for it."
   250	
   251	"Pannier," said Oryn. "Near side. It doesn't matter. He's the arms."
   252	
   253	Brom had never held one, as Cael saw from how he turned it over; but he had drawn carts and hauled rope all his life, and he found the iron stirrup at its nose, and put his boot in that, and bent and took the string in the fingers of both hands.
   254	
   255	He straightened his back, and the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch with a sound like a gate-latch. When he took his hands away there was a dark line across the pads of four fingers, and he wiped them down his breeches.
   256	
   257	Seln drew a bolt from the sleeve on the stock, and laid it in the groove.
   258	
   259	"Not from there," said Cael. "You'll have it jump. Get down outside, and lay it on the lip."
   260	
   261	It was the lip a third time. A hand's height had spoiled the spring of two hounds, and now it gave a man who had never shot a bow something to rest one on. Brom went back over it and knelt on the turf, box and all, and set the stock on the rolled edge and his cheek against the stock. There were four strides and a foot of white between the iron head of that bolt and the eye that Cael was looking into, and one foot more between the eye and Cael's own face.
   262	
   263	"Thirteen feet," said Karis. Her voice was a thread. She could no more have helped it than he could.
   264	
   265	"She throws low," said Oryn. "A hand, at that. Lay it on the top of the eye."
   266	
   267	"I've never shot one," said Brom.
   268	
   269	"I know you haven't. Hold your breath out, and squeeze it as you'd milk a goat."
   270	
   271	Cael did not move his hands. He had made the thing as still as he knew how to make it, and that was the whole of what he had to give. Nine fragments lay under his refusal, and not one of them could do what was wanted, which was to let a friend try. He looked across thirteen feet at one grey eye over a bow-stock, narrowed, and at a face that would have done anything on earth sooner than loose that string.
   272	
   273	"When you like," he said.
   274	
   275	There was a slap, and something went by his wrists with a wind on it.
   276	
   277	The head under his hands stopped pulling. There was no struggle in it: it was a weight that had been leaning against him, and then it was only a weight, and he was pressing a dead thing to the floor with all his strength.
   278	
   279	"Eight," said Karis.
   280	
   281	The pair stood. They had let go of two coats, and they stood as two had stood in the shallows of a river when the one in front of them died, with their heads level, waiting for something to follow.
   282	
   283	The jaws had not opened. Seln knelt, and put the flat of the narrow dull knife between the teeth at the hinge, and Cael took the upper jaw in both hands, and it was done as a trap is opened. Lira made one sound while they did it, and it was not loud.
   284	
   285	He had her knife in his hand when he stood up. He did not remember reaching for it.
   286	
   287	---
   288	
   289	They got her over the lip to the head of the lane.
   290	
   291	Brom carried her the four strides, on one arm, with the bow in the other hand, and Oryn was on her knees in the grass before Lira's back had touched it. Both her hands went round the arm, above the place and below it, as they had gone round a shin on a mountain, and her eyes shut.
   292	
   293	[[TIER-B: Oryn at the rim, hands on Lira's bitten arm, "Nothing… Nothing in my hands." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
   294	
   295	She took her hands away as though the arm had burnt them.
   296	
   297	Then she was a different person, and one that he had not met. She had a roll of linen out of her coat, the stiff kind that smelled of a pine wood, and her right knee on the inside of Lira's arm above the elbow, with all her weight on the knee.
   298	
   299	There was a good deal of blood, and it was black in that light. The teeth had met through the arm; he knew what that was, from the inside of his own calf, and he knew that his calf had been the smaller matter. He had seen her hurt in a dozen ways since a lane in Ardenmere, and he had never before seen so much of her on the outside.
   300	
   301	"You," said Oryn, to Karis. "Both hands. There, where my thumb is. Harder than that. You'll not hurt her worse than she's hurt."
   302	
   303	Karis put both hands where the thumb was, and leaned.
   304	
   305	The linen went on from the wrist upward, turn over turn, pulled with the healer's teeth at every third. Her lips kept no count, for there was nothing in her hands to count. She worked as the steading's woman had worked in a barn by two lanterns, quickly and without tenderness, and when the roll was done she tore the tail of it down the middle and knotted it.
   306	
   307	"Up. That stays above her heart." She took Lira's left wrist and laid it against Lira's own right collarbone, and shut Lira's right hand over it. "You hold that there. You don't let it down to balance, and you don't put it out if you fall. If you fall, you fall on your face."
   308	
   309	"The foot's gone," said Lira. Her teeth were knocking. "The right. I heard it."
   310	
   311	"I heard it too. It'll carry you flat. Put the whole sole down, every step, like a duck." Oryn got her own shoulder under Lira's right arm, and stood up with her. She was the shorter by half a head, and she took the weight as she would have taken a pannier.
   312	
   313	Then she looked at Cael. In that light her face was all planes, and there was a thing in it that he had not seen there at noon, when a count stopped in her mouth.
   314	
   315	"Move," Oryn said. "I can't fix this here. Move."
   316	
   317	He looked back once. The white lay behind them, a hand higher than the turf that he stood on, and it had not dimmed. One shape was stretched on it by the lip, four strides in, pale on pale, and eight others stood or sat among the feet of the stones. None of them had come a yard nearer while a bow was drawn and a girl was bound; they had watched it as the pale one had watched Brom from the rim.
   318	
   319	The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.
   320	
   321	"Seln in front," he said. "Karis behind him. Then you two. Brom, wind that thing again and keep it up. I'm last."
   322	
   323	*Two hundred and seven*, he thought, and they began to walk.

