 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 32 — The Loudest Thing
     2	
     3	There were two hundred and seven yards of lane and eight hounds, and he had both figures by heart before Brom had the string back.
     4	
     5	It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held both hands away from himself for a breath, as a man holds them who has picked up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
     6	
     7	They went down in the order that Cael had given. Seln was in front with the narrow knife, and Karis behind him with her hand on her coat. Then came the healer and Lira, as close as two people in one sack: Oryn on the right with her shoulder in Lira's armpit, and Lira's bound arm held up against her own collarbone, and the great boot coming down whole at every step like a duck's foot. Brom walked after them with the bow across his chest. Cael came last, four strides behind Brom's box, with Lira's knife in his hand and the read under his soles. He had the only eyes in the file that were not wanted for carrying somebody, and a rear guard needs room to see what it is guarding.
     8	
     9	He looked back at the third stride. The eight were coming down off the lip, one at a time, without haste, and going into the standing grass on either side of the lane. After that there was only grass.
    10	
    11	It was dusk in earnest now. The lane was a grey stroke going down between two dark walls as high as his hip. The west had a green light in it still, and behind him the floor lay on the crown of the swell like a moon that had come down to rest.
    12	
    13	"A hundred and sixty," he said.
    14	
    15	He said it low, for the file. His strides were yards, and he had paced this lane at noon behind a cord.
    16	
    17	The grass was moving.
    18	
    19	He had been waiting for it, and it was still a bad thing to see. On the south side, a few yards out from the lane, the seed-heads bent and stood and bent again in a slow line that went along beside the file. When he turned his head they were doing the same on the north. No shape showed in it anywhere; there was only the grass leaning, a little at a time, at exactly the pace of a girl putting down the whole of her foot.
    20	
    21	Lira stumbled, and the healer held her, and they stood for a breath. On both sides the grass stood too.
    22	
    23	"A hundred and fifty."
    24	
    25	He did what he had done from the east tier of a yard, and at the head of a gravel bar with four people hurt: he set out what he had counted, and looked for the seam in it.
    26	
    27	Nine had come home across that floor in no order, and one of them had sat down to scratch. Two had walked onto Brom's pole from the same place, a minute apart, with the second watching the first. Eight had stood by while a bow was loosed among them. They could have cut this lane ahead of the file at any stride of it, and they had not troubled to; they kept their station on the slowest thing that was walking, and waited for it to stop.
    28	
    29	At a ford, seven of their kind had learned inside a quarter of an hour to leave a silent body alone. These had learned nothing whatever since sunset, with one of their own lying dead on their own floor.
    30	
    31	They were careless. He turned the word over, and it fitted every count that he had. They had no cause to be anything else. Whatever walked over that line left its Path at a stone, and came on as a deer comes, or as a man with a stick; and in all the years that a pack had lain under that floor, no prey in this place had ever shouted.
    32	
    33	"A hundred and forty."
    34	
    35	*Give it one.* He had said that aloud on a gravel bar, and gone down thirty yards to do it. Whichever of them was nearest to a declaration moved first, and the rest fell in behind whatever moved; that was in his own handwriting, by Karis's dictation. He was the only thing between that floor and that stone that had one to give.
    36	
    37	He saw then what the room was that he had wanted on the floor, with his hands on a skull. It was a lane, and five people on it walking away from him. He had been carrying the answer since noon; he had stood at a boulder with a foot of Wind going out of him, and thought only of what he had found, and not at all of what it was for.
    38	
    39	It would not be enough to draw them off. They would come back to the lane when he fell quiet, or follow him to the stone and over it. On the far side of that stone there were four hurt people and the dark.
    40	
    41	He lengthened his stride and came up past Brom's elbow, and along the edge of the lane to the small figure behind Seln.
    42	
    43	"Karis. At the stone you'll have it back. How much of it?"
    44	
    45	She wasted no breath on why. She thought, walking, with her hand on the roll of paper.
    46	
    47	"I have set one point since the mountain, on a lamp-wick. It ought to be very nearly the whole." Her voice was small and dry. "Nine, as before, and the eight joins. I could hold them for a count of six and have something over. I was told not to spend it to the last copper where she was not, and she will be there."
    48	
    49	"Along the line. The stone in the middle, and as far each way as nine will reach. Set them and leave them dark. When the last of us is over, join them."
    50	
    51	"That is a wall. Across the only door we know of, against animals that hunt a declaration."
    52	
    53	"They hunt one. I've not seen one go through sixty yards of fire to reach it. I saw thirty head of horn turn off yours on a mountain."
    54	
    55	"That was derived."
    56	
    57	"Then it'll be derived twice."
    58	
    59	She was silent for four strides.
    60	
    61	"Who is last?"
    62	
    63	"I am. I've the rear."
    64	
    65	She turned her head and looked at him. It was nearly dark, and he could see only the pale shape of her face. He thought that she had heard something that was not about the order of a file.
    66	
    67	"Entered," said Karis.
    68	
    69	He fell back to the rear. As he passed Brom he said, "At the stone, turn round and keep that thing pointing in," and Brom grunted. Lira had her head down and her teeth shut. The healer's eyes went over him from a foot away, tired and level, as they went over everybody, and found nothing that wanted her hands.
    70	
    71	"A hundred and twenty," he said.
    72	
    73	---
    74	
    75	He counted them down by tens, and the grass kept pace on either hand.
    76	
    77	At a hundred Lira's bad foot dragged, and she made no sound; he knew it only from how the healer's shoulder dropped to take her. At ninety Brom said, without turning, that there was something in the lane behind them, and Cael looked. One of the eight had come out onto the trodden grass thirty yards back, and was sitting there with its tongue out. It got up when they had gone on ten strides, and came after, and sat again.
    78	
    79	"Eighty."
    80	
    81	He could see the stone. It was a paler thing at the lane's end, the size of a loaf. The mule stood beyond it with her long ears forward, hobbled, and in no fear at all; nothing in that grass wanted anything that she had.
    82	
    83	"Seventy."
    84	
    85	"Sixty," he said, and the grass on the south side opened.
    86	
    87	It was the dark-backed one. It came out low and from the flank, at the smallest thing in the file, with none of the idleness that it had shown on a lip. He had time to think that Karis walked alone, and that he had been a fool not to see what that would look like from the grass.
    88	
    89	She went over on her back with the animal on top of her, and he heard the breath go out of her. It struck for her throat, and she got both her hands there first, shut, one fist upon the other under her own chin, as a man of Thornwater had once got a hand between; and the jaws closed across the two fists together and drove them back into the place that they were guarding.
    90	
    91	She screamed.
    92	
    93	It was cut off almost as it began. It was still the loudest sound that anybody had made on that ground since the six of them crossed a stone; and not one head in the grass on either side so much as turned to it. They did not hunt by that.
    94	
    95	He saw all of it at once, as he saw a floor.
    96	
    97	Seln was five strides beyond her, turning, with the knife coming up; and between Seln and Karis one of the three that Cael had never told apart had stepped out into the lane and stood there, broadside, with its head level. Lira and the healer were five strides on the near side; Oryn had stopped dead, with her whole weight under Lira, and both of them were looking at the thing on the ground. Brom was a stride behind the two women, with the bow at his shoulder already.
    98	
    99	"No line," said Brom. It came out of him like a groan. "They're in it. She's under it. I've no line."
   100	
   101	Cael was four strides behind the box on Brom's back, and ten from Karis.
   102	
   103	He priced it. He had perhaps as long as it takes a jaw to finish closing.
   104	
   105	He could run. It was ten yards; but the animal would hear him come, as it had heard everything all evening and not cared, and it would have all the time it wanted.
   106	
   107	He could let Wind have him. It would crack like a whip over the lane and set him down beside her with his feet locked for a count, and it would bring eight animals to the ground where Karis was lying, with him standing in the middle of them unable to move.
   108	
   109	There was a third thing, and it was already lifting. It had lifted at sunset behind the other eight, as it lifted at every dusk, with the one offer that it ever made. It did not put anything into the air. It was made to take something out.
   110	
   111	It was sealed. He knew the seal's reason as he knew his own name, and he went through it in the half of a breath, since it was the one sum that he had never done carelessly. A defector's craft in his hands would tell whoever saw it where he had learned it. So: who could see?
   112	
   113	Lira could, and Brom, and Karis from under the thing's jaw; and Seln, who was looking straight back up the lane, and whose own stair it had come off. Those four knew what he was and had kept it for years. There was no bowman on a bank. There was nobody else nearer than two days' walk, and a pack of dogs carries no tales to a ferry.
   114	
   115	That left one. The healer knew nothing of him, and she spoke to every carter on seven roads. She had her back to him. A big man and a hundredweight of box stood between her and the place where he was, and her eyes were on Karis. She would see where he ended. She could not see where he began.
   116	
   117	*Yes*, he told it.
   118	
   119	He had not said so to that one since he crossed the Line.
   120	
   121	The hiss of the seed-heads against his coat stopped. That was how it came: the world went on, and he went out of it. His boots came down on trodden grass and gave him nothing back; he could not hear his own breath. He went wide of the lane on the south side, through stems that stood to his hip and bent for him and did not rustle, past the box on Brom's back and Brom's left shoulder. Brom's eye came round and slid over the place where he was, as an eye slides over one fence-post among forty, and then came back to it, since Brom knew. He passed Lira on her left hand, a yard out. Her head turned. She was looking at grass that leaned with nobody in it, and he saw her mouth open.
   122	
   123	The healer was on Lira's other side, a head lower, with the whole of a girl's weight on her shoulder, and she did not turn at all.
   124	
   125	It was ten strides. On the tenth he let it go, with his hands already on the animal's back.
   126	
   127	The sound of his own feet came back a beat late, like an echo off a wall. His hands had gone as cold as if he had held them in a spring. And the dark-backed one, which had heard nothing come and seen nothing come, shifted its grip to finish.
   128	
   129	He had its weight. He had been carrying a pace and a half of the world since the far lip, and he knew where every pound of that long body sat and which way it leaned. He took it by the loose skin behind the skull and by the root of the tail. He did not pull it off her, since a thing that is pulled bites down. He lifted the hind end as high as his chest and turned his whole back into it, as he would have thrown a sack onto a cart.
   130	
   131	Five bands of linen said *there*. He went through them.
   132	
   133	A hound with its hind feet off the ground has nothing to bite from. It let go of two fists to find the earth again, and by then it was in the air; and it went over his hip and into the standing grass on the south side, a full yard out, and he heard it land.
   134	
   135	Karis lay with her fists under her chin and her mouth open. No sound was coming out of it. Her eyes were on him, and they were quite clear. They went to his hands, and to the place ten strides back where he had been standing, and returned.
   136	
   137	"Seln," he said. "Carry her. The stone. Don't stop for me."
   138	
   139	"What are you—" said Brom.
   140	
   141	"I'm going to make a noise. Walk."
   142	
   143	"With what?" said Lira.
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	He did not answer her. He set his feet in the trodden grass as he had watched her set hers ten thousand times, with the lane across him and his face to the north.
   148	
   149	He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
   150	
   151	He stopped holding.
   152	
   153	The air cracked.
   154	
   155	It broke over the lane as it had broken over a river, and the grass went down flat behind him in a fan. He was twenty feet north of five people, in stems to his hip, and his boots had come down left foot first.
   156	
   157	The landing locked. *One*, and everything that he owned went dumb; there was a boy standing in a field with a knife, who could not have shifted a foot to save his life. He had priced that count before he spent it. The thrown one was on the south side, finding its legs, and the rest were strung along sixty yards of lane. The nearest of them was a good deal more than one count from his ankles.
   158	
   159	All along both sides of the lane, the grass had stopped moving at Lira's pace.
   160	
   161	It stood. Then it bent, everywhere at once, toward him.
   162	
   163	The count was over, and he had his nine.
   164	
   165	He drew breath for Pressure. It was a deeper breath than a healer's tape allowed, and his left side told him so from the breastbone to the spine. It gathered under his ribs for the better part of that breath, as it had always gathered, slowly, where anything alive might feel it coming. There was no opponent to send it through. He sent it straight down.
   166	
   167	The ground thumped like a drum. The grass lay over in a ring ten yards across, and the force of it came back up through his heels and his knees, as it came back off anything that would not move.
   168	
   169	Ember was next. He gave it the one place where he could carry it, which was the tip of Lira's knife; and for the first time since the Line he did not stop at the setting. It lit. A seed of yellow stood on the steel, and then a flame as long as his thumb, burning on nothing, at the end of his arm.
   170	
   171	He could see them.
   172	
   173	They had come up out of the grass: there were low grey shapes on both sides of the lane and in it, and every head was turned to him, and in every head there were two small green fires where the flame looked back. He counted the pairs. He could not have helped it.
   174	
   175	Eight.
   176	
   177	Something bright and quick stirred in his right arm and asked what it had asked of every sill and rim and arris since the day it came to him. *Edge?*
   178	
   179	*Yes*, he told that one too. He had never said so before. Along the knife's cutting side a line of white stood out a hair clear of the steel, as a tall exact person had once made one stand on a lath by saying that it did; and it rang. There was nothing for it to cut. It sang all the same, thin and high, like a wet finger going round the rim of a glass.
   180	

