/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md | sed -n '181,360p'" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
   181	He had a light in his fist and a noise at the end of it, and he was the only thing on that whole hillside that had either.
   182	
   183	He ran north.
   184	
   185	Behind him he heard Lira's voice, once, and it was not his name; it was "Go!" and it was not said to him.
   186	
   187	They came after him, and they did not come as they had come all evening. There was nothing idle in them now. They came through the grass as seven had come across a river to a seed of fire on a rock, flat out, with the leaders drawing together and the rest falling in behind; and he had the sound of them on both hands, a dry rushing, like a wind that had found its direction.
   188	
   189	There was one thing more that he had refused at every doorway and coping since a river, and he let that off its hold with the rest. Anchor fixed nothing, for he had no point on that hill that he could afford to nail to the ground. It only looked, west, as a dog let off a chain looks first at the gate. It would be within ten feet of a thing before it could tell him what was there.
   190	
   191	It was forty yards to the rut. He counted them, with the flame streaming back over his wrist and the edge singing. At the fortieth the ground went out from under his left foot, and he was in it to the knee.
   192	
   193	---
   194	
   195	West was the stone. That was why he turned east.
   196	
   197	The rut ran up the swell toward the floor, a little south of east, and every stride that he took along it was a stride away from a lane with a lame girl on it. He put his back to five people and went up the hill the wrong way, with eight animals pouring into the channel behind him.
   198	
   199	He had kept the heaviest thing for a place that would hold it.
   200	
   201	A Storm lane wanted the air, and to have the air he must give up the ground; at that weight the read would carry one or the other, never both. He let the turf go. It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half of the world that he had walked in since the far lip went out from under his soles. He was a boy running up a ditch in the dark, with a flame in front of his face and nothing to tell him where his feet would land.
   202	
   203	He laid the lane down the rut behind him.
   204	
   205	It was a corridor of air the length of a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other, and everything in it went downhill at once. The pale grass stood along both lips of the rut with its seed heavy on it. The lane stripped it. Seed and chaff and dry stem went down that channel in a flood, as thick as meal out of a sack, straight into the faces of whatever was coming up.
   206	
   207	He heard them meet it. He let the lane go, and paid.
   208	
   209	*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
   210	
   211	At the tenth count he laid the second.
   212	
   213	It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
   214	
   215	It opened again. He felt it go, like wet cloth parting, and after that every stride on that side had a hot place in it.
   216	
   217	*Eight. Nine. Ten.*
   218	
   219	He laid the third. It was as many as he had ever laid; he did not know what a fourth would do to him, and it was not the night to learn. The rut behind him was a river of chaff. Twelve counts, for the third. His own breath reached him late.
   220	
   221	He had counted his strides from the place where he dropped into the rut, and he was at a hundred and thirty when he heard it.
   222	
   223	"*Back!*"
   224	
