   238	
   239	Then she raised her hands.
   240	
   241	Both, in front of her, a little apart, palms in, the way she raised them for a reading with nobody between them — the count, he knew, running in her own hands. She held them there. Then she stepped over the stone.
   242	
   243	One stride. Inside. Her hands stayed up, and her face did what it had done at the rim over Lira's pouring arm and over Brom's forearm on the first morning: the count stopping at the stride. Not ending. Stopping. She stood inside the line with her hands raised to nothing, and looked at them, and looked at the stone behind her heel.
   244	
   245	One stride. Out. Her hands had not been lowered; they had been held, and her eyes went to the place, and found it, and her mouth moved, and he knew the word without hearing it because he had heard it on this road before.
   246	
   247	She did it again. In: nothing. Out: back.
   248	
   249	The third time, slower, with a pause inside long enough for him to count twelve — standing on the grass with her hands up in the grey and the whole of a dead-Path circle in front of her and a den at the far side of it, alone, checking whether a rule that had never once failed anybody who kept it would fail her if she stood in it long enough; and it did not, and she stepped out, and it came back. And the fourth time, as if three were an anecdote and four were a finding: in, hands raised, nothing; out, hands raised, the current back at the stride, exactly, the clock starting again without having lost the time — being checked, on the second dawn, by the one of them who had walked past this bend eleven times in two years and never stepped off the road, and had now stepped over its stone eight times, four in and four out, the way Karis had paced the circle: with a count, and nobody watching, and the count kept in her own hands.
   250	
   251	He did not write it down.
   252	
   253	The book lay on his chest with his hand on it and the pencil in the spine, and he kept it shut. It was hers. She had not said it, and she would not want it said yet; she would say it when she had it as a finding and not as a morning, and until then it was not his to file. So he lay in the rut with the calf bound and the ribs stopping his breath at the tape, and watched the healer stand at the line with her hands up in the grey and the stone at her feet, and let her be the only one awake, until the light came onto the floor behind her and went white along its whole width at once, and she lowered her hands, and stood a moment longer looking at them, and then came back along the road to the camp, quiet, as a carter walks quiet ground, and lay down in her place by Lira's arm.

